  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 321 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nAn amplified trough will develop over the western half of the\ncountry early next week. This will bring showers to southern WI\nat times through the work week. Our first likely chance for\nshowers will arrive on Monday afternoon along a warm front as \nlow pressure approaches from Nebraska. With the warm air\nadvection, vorticity advection, and weak low level jet, we\ncannot rule out some elevated thunderstorms. \n\nThe thing to watch as this forecast comes closer in time is the\nmid level vorticity pattern. We have a series of upper lows that\nwill be swinging through the general upper trough. The upper\nlows/shortwaves are going to drive the strength and location of\nthunderstorms on Tuesday. With fairly significant differences\nbetween the models at this time, there is a great deal of\nuncertainty on our thunderstorm chances for the Tue-Wed\ntimeframe. \n\nDepending on how quickly that upper low moves out, we may have\nchances for showers lingering later in the work week. \n\nCronce",
